A host's keyed packages evaluate each module once, not once an extension.

A keyed package's extensions already share one factory each across
folders, but jiti gives each extension's import a module cache of its
own, so a library two of its extensions import is evaluated once for
each. A keyed package's loads now share the modules the package has
finished, so each file they reach is evaluated once for the package.
Node's module cache is neither read nor written, and every other
package's loads keep their modules their own.

Each load evaluates in a cache of its own over the package's finished
modules, and what it finishes joins those only once it succeeds, so no
load is given a module another is still evaluating, or one a failed load
left part-way. An import a module makes while its load is under way is
that load's: it shares what the load has finished, is given part-way, as
jiti gives it, what the load is still evaluating, and fails with it. An
import an extension makes as it runs, once its load has ended, is given
only finished modules too: one of a module the package hasn't finished
evaluates it in a cache of its own over its load's and the package's
finished modules, joining them once it succeeds, and only where each load
it began from joined its own. Two such imports of one module at once from
one load share one evaluation. Of two evaluations at once that each
finish a copy of one module, the first to end joins the package's and the
other joins none of what it finished, so no load is given both. What
jiti doesn't evaluate itself, a builtin, a data URL, JSON or a module it
leaves to Node, stays jiti's own.

A package's loads take turns, so two loads don't both evaluate a library
they share. A reload, and a link moving off a copy, give the package's
next load fresh modules and let go of the loads waiting behind one that
never ends; those import as an unkeyed package's do. A load that holds
its turn past ten seconds lets the loads after it go on without waiting,
sharing what the package has finished, until it ends. A factory is kept
only from a load given modules still its package's, so none from a copy
a link has moved off, or from modules let go of.

