+// jiti gives each import of an extension a module cache of its own, so a
+// library its extensions share was evaluated once for each extension
+// importing it. A keyed package's loads share the modules they finish
+// instead, so each file they reach, inside the package or not, is evaluated
+// once for the package. Those modules are the package's own and never
+// Node's, so no other package's load reads them, and its loads read nothing
+// the host put in Node's. A reload, or the package's last shared extension
+// resolving elsewhere, lets go of them, so the package's next load begins
+// anew, and a load keeps the modules it began with, so nothing it finishes
+// reaches a load begun after.
+//
+// jiti hands an import any module its cache holds, finished or not, so no
+// module is shared before it has finished. Each load evaluates in a cache
